                <pb facs="0480" n="366" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg366"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5496: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-09-06T12:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 6,
                                1924—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received September 6—8:15
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>325. My 318, September 2, 5 p.m., last paragraph. Naval attaché returned
                    yesterday from Harbin and Mukden, reports after careful inspection he could find
                    no evidence of Marshal Chang Tso-lin intending to move in immediate future. His
                    attitude appeared to be one of watchful waiting.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
